=== the rule, chosen by a declared criterion rather than by taste ===
  requirement: sensitivity >= 80% on known natural structures
  among those, take the STRICTEST (largest k, then smallest d)
  currently shipped rule CONFIRMED(k=3, d=0): sensitivity 0.8%
  -> CONFIRMED(k=3, d=20) at sensitivity 89.2%
     a certificate can then say: 'passes a criterion that recognises 89% of known natural structures'

=== RELATIVE tolerance r = bp_dist / n_pairs(T): comparable across target sizes ===
  (250 natives, 250 randoms; r=1 is as wrong as predicting nothing, so r>=1 is vacuous)
      r                    k=1                   k=2                   k=3
              sens      random      sens      random      sens      random
   0.00     37.2%        0.0%     7.6%        0.0%     0.8%        0.0%
   0.10     42.0%        0.0%    13.6%        0.0%     2.0%        0.0%
   0.20     55.6%        0.0%    16.8%        0.0%     4.0%        0.0%
   0.30     64.8%        0.0%    22.4%        0.0%     6.8%        0.0%
   0.50     75.6%        0.0%    58.8%        0.0%    39.2%        0.0%
   0.75     79.2%        1.2%    67.6%        0.8%    55.6%        0.4%
   1.00     90.0%        8.0%    80.0%        2.8%    70.8%        1.2%

  usable under the SAME declared rule, restricted to meaningful r < 1: NONE
  -> Once the tolerance is made comparable across target sizes, NO rule in this family
     recognises 80% of known structures while rejecting random sequences. The absolute-d
     winner (k=1, d=8) was an artefact of averaging a mild tolerance on big targets with a
     vacuous one on small targets.

=== a different judged quantity: CROSSING-PAIR RECALL (the pairs that make it a pseudoknot) ===
  native means: pkiss 0.85, probknot 0.62, shapeknots 0.63
  (250 natives, 250 randoms)
    tau                    k=1                   k=2                   k=3
              sens      random      sens      random      sens      random
   1.00     84.0%        0.4%    63.6%        0.4%    43.2%        0.4%
   0.90     84.0%        0.4%    63.6%        0.4%    43.2%        0.4%
   0.80     86.4%        0.4%    66.4%        0.4%    52.0%        0.4%
   0.67     87.6%        0.8%    67.6%        0.8%    52.0%        0.4%
   0.50     90.4%        2.8%    70.0%        2.0%    55.2%        1.6%

  usable under the SAME declared rule: [(1, 1.0, 0.84, 0.004), (1, 0.9, 0.84, 0.004), (1, 0.8, 0.864, 0.004), (1, 0.67, 0.876, 0.008), (1, 0.5, 0.904, 0.028)]
  -> crossing-pair recall SEPARATES where exact match did not: k=1, tau=1.0 at 84.0% sensitivity / 0.4% random false-accept.
     A certificate could then say 'reproduces the pseudoknot stems under 1 of 3 independent predictors, a criterion that recognises 84% of known structures'.

=== combined rule: nested skeleton by an EXACT judge AND crossing stems by the panel ===
  wild-type sequences parsed and matched to targets: 186/250
  native skeleton recovered by ViennaRNA MFE alone: 20/186 (10.8%)
    (a native really does form a pseudoknot, so its MFE need not equal the nested reduction -- this is the number that decides whether the two parts compose)

    tau   k  combined sens  combined random
   1.00   1          8.6%            0.0%
   0.90   1          8.6%            0.0%
   0.80   1          8.6%            0.0%
   0.67   1          8.6%            0.0%
   0.50   1          9.1%            0.0%

  usable combined rules under the SAME declared bar: NONE
  -> the two parts do NOT compose: requiring the nested skeleton to be the ViennaRNA MFE
     costs most of the sensitivity, because a native's MFE is not its nested reduction.
     The defensible criterion therefore stays crossing-stem recall alone, and a
     certificate must say that it speaks about the KNOT, not the whole structure.

